WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
Kernel update breakage is a common Linux GPU issue.

00:00:03.374 --> 00:00:09.989
A new kernel may require driver modules to be rebuilt, and missing headers can prevent that rebuild.

00:00:09.989 --> 00:00:15.149
DKMS can help, but it still depends on compatible packages and kernel headers.

00:00:15.149 --> 00:00:25.138
If a system fails after a kernel update, check the running kernel, installed headers, module build status, and driver logs before making broad changes.

00:00:25.138 --> 00:00:30.430
Secure Boot can block unsigned or improperly signed kernel modules from loading.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.730
The result may look like the driver is installed but unavailable.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:39.427
Some environments require module signing or Secure Boot policy changes.

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:44.256
The right approach depends on organizational policy and system ownership.

00:00:44.256 --> 00:00:49.218
Treat this as a controlled configuration decision, not a random workaround.

00:00:49.218 --> 00:00:57.950
Display conflicts, black screens, missing device permissions, and containers that cannot see the GPU each point to different layers.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:01.787
Display problems may involve graphics stack configuration.

00:01:01.787 --> 00:01:06.483
Permission problems may involve device nodes, groups, or service users.

00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:12.437
Container issues may involve runtime flags, toolkit configuration, or image compatibility.

00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:19.516
Version mismatch errors often indicate an unsupported combination of driver, library, runtime, and package.

00:01:19.516 --> 00:01:24.609
Deciding when to rollback, reinstall, or rebuild is an operational judgement.

00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:28.380
Rollback is attractive when a known-good snapshot exists.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:34.598
Reinstall may be reasonable when the package state is confused but the base system is healthy.

00:01:34.598 --> 00:01:41.148
Rebuild may be faster when experiments have left the machine inconsistent and documentation exists.

00:01:41.148 --> 00:01:48.623
The safest troubleshooting habit is to preserve evidence, change one variable at a time, and document the result.
